  "full_response": "Supervised, unsupervised, and reinforcement learning are three fundamental paradigms in machine learning, each with distinct approaches and use cases. Here’s a breakdown of their differences:\n\n---\n\n### **1. Supervised Learning**\n**Definition:**\n- The model is trained on a **labeled dataset**, where each input (feature) is paired with the correct output (label). The goal is to learn a mapping from inputs to outputs.\n\n**Key Characteristics:**\n- **Labeled Data:** Requires pre-labeled data (e.g., images labeled as \"cat\" or \"dog\").\n- **Goal:** Predict the output for new, unseen inputs (regression or classification).\n- **Feedback:** Direct feedback is given during training (e.g., \"this prediction is correct/incorrect\").\n- **Examples:**\n  - **Classification:** Spam detection (email → spam/ham), image recognition (image → object class).\n  - **Regression:** House price prediction (features → price), stock market forecasting.\n\n**Algorithms:**\n- Linear Regression, Logistic Regression, Decision Trees, Random Forest, Support Vector Machines (SVM), Neural Networks.\n\n**Pros:**\n- High accuracy when labeled data is abundant.\n- Clear evaluation metrics (e.g., accuracy, precision, recall).\n\n**Cons:**\n- Requires large amounts of labeled data (expensive/time-consuming to obtain).\n- Struggles with tasks where labels are ambiguous or unavailable.\n\n---\n\n### **2. Unsupervised Learning**\n**Definition:**\n- The model is trained on an **unlabeled dataset**, where the goal is to find hidden patterns or intrinsic structures in the data without explicit guidance.\n\n**Key Characteristics:**\n- **Unlabeled Data:** No predefined labels; the model explores the data autonomously.\n- **Goal:** Discover underlying structure (e.g., clustering, dimensionality reduction, anomaly detection).\n- **Feedback:** No explicit feedback; the model infers patterns.\n- **Examples:**\n  - **Clustering:** Customer segmentation (group similar customers), document clustering.\n  - **Dimensionality Reduction:** PCA for feature extraction, t-SNE for visualization.\n  - **Anomaly Detection:** Fraud detection (identify unusual transactions).\n  - **Association:** Market basket analysis (e.g., \"people who buy X also buy Y\").\n\n**Algorithms:**\n- K-Means, Hierarchical Clustering, DBSCAN, Principal Component Analysis (PCA), Autoencoders, Apriori (for association rules).\n\n**Pros:**\n- Works with unlabeled data (",
